    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3/3 tests passing).\n\n## What I did\n\nAddress normalization now runs on every owner creation, added as a small cohesive unit rather than by growing any existing class.\n\n**New file \u2014 `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java`** (one public method, no surrounding class complexity):\n- Trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE` (token-based, via a small lookup map).\n- Mutates the `OwnerFieldsDto` in place via `@Val`, exactly like the existing `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`/`NormalizeOwnerEmail` steps.\n\n**Wiring \u2014 `owners.POST.yml`**: inserted a `normalizeAddress` step between `normalizeEmail` and `rejectDuplicate`.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\nI changed **zero existing methods** \u2014 the cost formula's biggest lever (`WMC_other` of a large class) never applies, because the only code function I touched is a brand-new single-method class with no siblings. Two files touched total (the new class + the YAML).\n\nThis works because normalizing the address *on the stored DTO before `BuildOwner`* makes every downstream consumer see the normalized form automatically:\n- **Store/return**: the persisted `Owner.address` is normalized, so `GET` returns `\"12 MAIN STREET\"` / `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`.\n- **Household duplicate detection** (`RejectDuplicateHousehold`) and **shared household id** (`HouseholdId.of` via `OwnerMapper`) both read the already-normalized address \u2014 no edits needed to those classes.\n- **Blank-after-normalization**: `RequireOwnerFields` runs first and its `isBlank()` check rejects a whitespace-only address (`\"   \"` \u2192 400). Since normalization only removes whitespace and expands tokens, \"blank after normalization\" is exactly \"blank before,\" so the existing required-field check already enforces it correctly.",
